To find $g(3)$, we first need $f^{-1}(x)$, the inverse of $f(x) = \frac{x+5}{3}$. To find the inverse, we set $y = f(x)$ and solve for $x$:
\begin{align*}
y &= \frac{x+5}{3} \\
3y &= x + 5 \\
x &= 3y - 5
\end{align*}
Thus, $f^{-1}(x) = 3x - 5$. Now, we can find $g(3)$ using the definition of $g(x)$:
\begin{align*}
g(x) &= \frac{1}{f^{-1}(x) + 1} \\
g(3) &= \frac{1}{f^{-1}(3) + 1} \\
&= \frac{1}{3(3) - 5 + 1} \\
&= \frac{1}{9 - 5 + 1} \\
&= \frac{1}{5}
\end{align*}
<answer>\frac{1}{5}</answer><|im_end|>
